James Kanze wrote:
> Why not XML, while we're at it:-) ?
 > ...
> The encodings of ASN.1 that I've seen tend to be rather heavy.
> Sort of a binary equivalent to XML.  On the other hand, ASN.1 is
> a very nice description language, for documenting the format.
> And something a little more complete that XDR would seem in
> order.

I've found XML to be unnecessarily redundant. I mentioned
s-expressions since they allow data to be structured if needed, don't
require a lot of overhead if it's not, have a very small number of
reserved characters, and have such a simple grammar that writing a
parser is almost trivial.  A general purpose function for converting
arbitrary structs to and from an s-expression representation *would*
probably require some sort of language like ASN.1.  It would also
be possible to write code that compiles a struct declaration into
conversion code for that particular type, but probably not wise. The
parser for that would be...somewhat less trivial, at the very least.
Bitfields and typedefs in particular would be kind of hairy.  Now that
I think about it, bitfields might be a problem for any encoding, since
they're only used if BITFIELDS is #defined.

I haven't used ASN.1 personally, so I can't comment too much on that
particularly. Looking at DER, the only real problem I see is that
since it uses offsets, corruption of an offset would make it just as
hard to find the start of the next triplet as it is to find the start
of the next object in the current system.  It probably would be a step
up in terms of portability, though.


